1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:04,468
About a quarter of the SAT Reading
and Writing section is grammar.

2
00:00:04,718 --> 00:00:09,432
And every one of those questions comes
from the same short list of rules.

3
00:00:10,946 --> 00:00:12,140
Thirteen rules.

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,604
In this video, we'll go
through all thirteen,

5
00:00:14,624 --> 00:00:18,405
and for most of them, you'll get
an SAT-style question to try.

6
00:00:18,425 --> 00:00:21,583
But first, the biggest mistake
students make on these questions:

7
00:00:21,603 --> 00:00:24,139
picking the answer that sounds right.

8
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:27,155
On the SAT, sounding right isn't the test.

9
00:00:27,175 --> 00:00:28,512
The rules are.

10
00:00:28,532 --> 00:00:30,477
The rules split into two groups.

11
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:34,532
Part one is punctuation, which the
College Board calls Boundaries.

12
00:00:34,854 --> 00:00:37,762
Part two is grammar,
like agreement, verbs,

13
00:00:37,782 --> 00:00:40,298
and modifiers, which it
calls Form, Structure,

14
00:00:40,318 --> 00:00:41,332
and Sense.

15
00:00:41,352 --> 00:00:42,352
Part one.

16
00:00:42,388 --> 00:00:43,424
Boundaries.

17
00:00:43,709 --> 00:00:48,674
These questions are all about where
one idea ends and the next one begins.

18
00:00:48,706 --> 00:00:49,956
Rule one.

19
00:00:50,099 --> 00:00:56,222
Know what a complete sentence is, because
almost every punctuation rule depends

20
00:00:56,242 --> 00:00:56,816
on it.

21
00:00:56,836 --> 00:00:59,887
A complete sentence, also
called an independent clause,

22
00:00:59,907 --> 00:01:03,066
has a subject, a verb,
and a complete thought.

23
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:04,550
It can stand on its own.

24
00:01:04,581 --> 00:01:05,867
The bridge was closed.

25
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:07,132
That's complete.

26
00:01:07,152 --> 00:01:08,918
The subject is bridge.

27
00:01:08,938 --> 00:01:10,759
The verb is was closed.

28
00:01:10,779 --> 00:01:12,636
Now add one word.

29
00:01:13,029 --> 00:01:15,243
Because the bridge was closed.

30
00:01:15,493 --> 00:01:17,402
Suddenly it isn't complete.

31
00:01:17,422 --> 00:01:18,581
You're waiting for the rest.

32
00:01:18,601 --> 00:01:20,351
That's a dependent clause.

33
00:01:20,404 --> 00:01:24,170
Words like because, although,
when, and while do that.

34
00:01:24,190 --> 00:01:26,527
So here's the test you'll use all video:

35
00:01:26,547 --> 00:01:28,884
could this part stand alone as a sentence?

36
00:01:28,904 --> 00:01:30,760
If it can, it's independent.

37
00:01:30,821 --> 00:01:31,873
Rule two.

38
00:01:31,893 --> 00:01:35,337
You can't join two complete
sentences with just a comma.

39
00:01:35,357 --> 00:01:39,122
That's called a comma splice, and it's
one of the most common traps on these

40
00:01:39,142 --> 00:01:40,098
questions.

41
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:44,348
There are three main ways to
join two complete sentences.

42
00:01:44,368 --> 00:01:45,740
Use a period.

43
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:46,883
Use a semicolon.

44
00:01:46,903 --> 00:01:49,903
Or use a comma plus a connecting word:

45
00:01:50,046 --> 00:01:55,740
for, and, nor, but, or, yet, and so.

46
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:58,118
A lot of people remember those as FANBOYS.

47
00:01:58,191 --> 00:01:59,191
Let's try one.

48
00:01:59,655 --> 00:02:01,670
The octopus has three hearts.

49
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:06,324
Two of them pump blood through the blank
pumps blood to the rest of the body.

50
00:02:06,344 --> 00:02:08,272
Pause here and pick your answer.

51
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:14,415
First, find the two complete sentences.

52
00:02:14,435 --> 00:02:16,559
Two of them pump blood through the gills.

53
00:02:16,579 --> 00:02:17,023
Complete.

54
00:02:17,043 --> 00:02:19,702
The third pumps blood
to the rest of the body.

55
00:02:19,722 --> 00:02:21,079
Also complete.

56
00:02:21,396 --> 00:02:24,447
A puts only a comma
between them: comma splice.

57
00:02:24,467 --> 00:02:26,860
C has no punctuation: run-on.

58
00:02:27,039 --> 00:02:30,324
D puts the comma after and,
which is the wrong spot.

59
00:02:30,361 --> 00:02:34,412
Choice B puts a semicolon
between two complete sentences.

60
00:02:34,432 --> 00:02:35,644
That's the answer.

61
00:02:35,664 --> 00:02:36,949
Rule three.

62
00:02:37,413 --> 00:02:40,751
A semicolon works exactly like a period.

63
00:02:40,771 --> 00:02:44,510
Whatever is on both sides has
to be a complete sentence.

64
00:02:44,530 --> 00:02:49,188
The experiment failed, semicolon,
the team still published its results.

65
00:02:49,208 --> 00:02:52,601
Both sides can stand alone,
so the semicolon works.

66
00:02:52,674 --> 00:02:53,547
Now this one.

67
00:02:53,567 --> 00:02:56,496
Although the experiment failed, semicolon,

68
00:02:56,567 --> 00:02:58,174
the team learned a lot.

69
00:02:58,353 --> 00:03:02,082
The first part starts with
although, so it can't stand alone.

70
00:03:02,102 --> 00:03:04,246
The semicolon is wrong.

71
00:03:04,317 --> 00:03:06,206
This sentence needs a comma.

72
00:03:06,226 --> 00:03:08,776
Quick check: swap the
semicolon for a period.

73
00:03:08,796 --> 00:03:11,313
If you get two real sentences, it works.

74
00:03:11,333 --> 00:03:15,741
The only other use is separating list
items that already have commas inside

75
00:03:15,761 --> 00:03:16,317
them.

76
00:03:16,337 --> 00:03:17,480
Rule four.

77
00:03:17,551 --> 00:03:20,587
A colon needs a complete
sentence before it.

78
00:03:20,658 --> 00:03:24,337
After it comes a list, an
example, or an explanation.

79
00:03:24,361 --> 00:03:25,647
Here's the trap.

80
00:03:27,253 --> 00:03:31,734
The decree was written
in, colon, hieroglyphic,

81
00:03:31,754 --> 00:03:33,448
Demotic, and Greek.

82
00:03:33,468 --> 00:03:34,627
Written in what?

83
00:03:34,647 --> 00:03:37,126
The part before the colon isn't complete,

84
00:03:37,146 --> 00:03:38,770
so the colon is wrong.

85
00:03:38,790 --> 00:03:41,448
No colons right after
a verb, a preposition,

86
00:03:41,468 --> 00:03:44,218
or words like including and such as.

87
00:03:44,357 --> 00:03:45,357
One more thing.

88
00:03:45,643 --> 00:03:48,536
A single dash can do
the same job as a colon.

89
00:03:48,679 --> 00:03:53,142
It can introduce a list or an
explanation after a complete sentence.

90
00:03:53,231 --> 00:03:54,266
Try this one.

91
00:03:54,302 --> 00:04:00,157
The Rosetta Stone carries the same decree
written in three blank hieroglyphic,

92
00:04:00,177 --> 00:04:02,070
Demotic, and ancient Greek.

93
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,370
Pause and try it.

94
00:04:06,608 --> 00:04:10,838
Before the blank: The Rosetta Stone
carries the same decree written in three

95
00:04:10,858 --> 00:04:11,695
scripts.

96
00:04:11,715 --> 00:04:12,838
Complete sentence.

97
00:04:12,858 --> 00:04:14,858
After it: a list.

98
00:04:14,894 --> 00:04:19,226
A complete sentence followed by a
list is exactly what a colon is for.

99
00:04:19,246 --> 00:04:23,012
B uses a semicolon, but the list
after it isn't a complete sentence.

100
00:04:23,032 --> 00:04:25,190
C has no punctuation at all.

101
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:27,048
And D creates a comma splice.

102
00:04:27,068 --> 00:04:28,415
The answer is A.

103
00:04:28,435 --> 00:04:29,344
Rule five.

104
00:04:29,364 --> 00:04:33,451
Words like however, therefore, moreover,

105
00:04:33,471 --> 00:04:36,042
and for example are transitions.

106
00:04:36,114 --> 00:04:39,987
They are not connecting
words like and or but,

107
00:04:40,007 --> 00:04:43,929
so they can't join two complete
sentences with just commas.

108
00:04:43,949 --> 00:04:45,056
Compare these.

109
00:04:45,127 --> 00:04:47,841
The test was hard, but she finished early.

110
00:04:48,056 --> 00:04:51,250
That's correct, because
but is one of the FANBOYS.

111
00:04:51,270 --> 00:04:53,607
The test was hard, however,
she finished early.

112
00:04:53,627 --> 00:04:54,893
That's a comma splice.

113
00:04:54,913 --> 00:04:56,969
However can't connect two sentences.

114
00:04:56,989 --> 00:05:00,111
The fix is a semicolon or
a period before however,

115
00:05:00,131 --> 00:05:01,989
and a comma after it.

116
00:05:02,274 --> 00:05:04,969
The test was hard, semicolon, however,

117
00:05:04,989 --> 00:05:06,957
comma, she finished early.

118
00:05:06,977 --> 00:05:07,743
One note.

119
00:05:07,763 --> 00:05:11,850
Commas around however are fine when it
sits in the middle of a single sentence,

120
00:05:11,870 --> 00:05:14,421
like, the results, however, were clear.

121
00:05:14,441 --> 00:05:18,763
The problem is only when however
sits between two complete sentences.

122
00:05:18,838 --> 00:05:19,838
Your turn.

123
00:05:20,552 --> 00:05:27,640
Many people assume that bats are
blank every bat species can see,

124
00:05:27,713 --> 00:05:33,177
and many species also use
echolocation to navigate in the dark.

125
00:05:33,203 --> 00:05:34,453
Pause and try it.

126
00:05:37,691 --> 00:05:40,885
Both sides are complete sentences,
and however sits between them.

127
00:05:40,905 --> 00:05:43,064
So we need a semicolon before however,

128
00:05:43,084 --> 00:05:44,564
and a comma after it.

129
00:05:44,584 --> 00:05:46,599
A and D are comma splices.

130
00:05:46,619 --> 00:05:47,992
C has nothing before however.

131
00:05:48,012 --> 00:05:49,263
The answer is B.

132
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:50,810
Rule six.

133
00:05:50,989 --> 00:05:54,612
Extra information, the kind you could
delete without breaking the sentence,

134
00:05:54,632 --> 00:05:56,575
gets wrapped in a matching pair.

135
00:05:56,595 --> 00:05:57,397
Two commas.

136
00:05:57,417 --> 00:05:58,148
Two dashes.

137
00:05:58,168 --> 00:05:59,632
Or two parentheses.

138
00:05:59,670 --> 00:06:04,757
This is what the SAT loves to
test: the pair has to match.

139
00:06:04,777 --> 00:06:07,865
If it opens with a comma,
it closes with a comma.

140
00:06:07,885 --> 00:06:10,848
If it opens with a dash,
it closes with a dash.

141
00:06:10,868 --> 00:06:12,047
Here's how to check.

142
00:06:12,261 --> 00:06:13,670
Delete the extra part.

143
00:06:13,690 --> 00:06:16,527
If what's left is still a
complete, correct sentence,

144
00:06:16,547 --> 00:06:18,296
you've found the right boundaries.

145
00:06:18,318 --> 00:06:19,012
Try it.

146
00:06:19,032 --> 00:06:24,655
Mary Anning, a largely self-taught fossil
collector from Lyme Regis blank made

147
00:06:24,675 --> 00:06:28,925
some of the most important fossil
discoveries of the early nineteenth century.

148
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:30,260
Pause and try it.

149
00:06:33,498 --> 00:06:37,442
The extra information opens
with a comma after Mary Anning,

150
00:06:37,462 --> 00:06:39,478
so it has to close with a comma.

151
00:06:39,498 --> 00:06:43,584
Delete the middle: Mary Anning made
some of the most important fossil

152
00:06:43,604 --> 00:06:46,498
discoveries of the early
nineteenth century.

153
00:06:46,605 --> 00:06:47,429
Clean.

154
00:06:47,449 --> 00:06:49,750
A mixes a comma with a dash.

155
00:06:49,770 --> 00:06:53,484
C uses a semicolon, which leaves
pieces that can't stand alone.

156
00:06:53,592 --> 00:06:55,250
D never closes the pair.

157
00:06:55,270 --> 00:06:56,769
The answer is B.

158
00:06:56,846 --> 00:07:00,253
Rule seven is about where
punctuation does not go.

159
00:07:00,273 --> 00:07:03,773
Plenty of wrong answers add
punctuation where none belongs.

160
00:07:03,793 --> 00:07:07,721
Never put a single comma
between a subject and its verb.

161
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:10,308
The students who finished early, comma,

162
00:07:10,328 --> 00:07:11,613
left the room.

163
00:07:11,650 --> 00:07:12,487
Wrong.

164
00:07:12,507 --> 00:07:15,201
The subject is the students
who finished early.

165
00:07:15,221 --> 00:07:16,486
The verb is left.

166
00:07:16,506 --> 00:07:18,185
No comma between them.

167
00:07:18,212 --> 00:07:20,120
Same with a verb and what follows it.

168
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:24,228
The study found, comma,
that sleep improves memory.

169
00:07:24,248 --> 00:07:25,248
Found what?

170
00:07:25,318 --> 00:07:27,085
That sleep improves memory.

171
00:07:27,105 --> 00:07:28,120
No comma.

172
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:32,426
And no commas around a name when the
name tells you which person you mean.

173
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:37,513
In 1993, novelist Toni
Morrison won the Nobel Prize in

174
00:07:37,533 --> 00:07:38,533
Literature.

175
00:07:38,748 --> 00:07:42,890
No commas around Toni Morrison, because
the name tells you which novelist.

176
00:07:42,929 --> 00:07:43,929
Part two.

177
00:07:44,001 --> 00:07:46,929
Form, Structure, and Sense.

178
00:07:47,286 --> 00:07:50,158
These questions test
whether words fit together:

179
00:07:50,178 --> 00:07:53,087
subjects and verbs, pronouns and nouns,

180
00:07:53,107 --> 00:07:56,679
verbs and time, and modifiers
and what they describe.

181
00:07:56,742 --> 00:07:58,028
Rule eight.

182
00:07:58,278 --> 00:08:01,044
A verb has to match its subject.

183
00:08:01,064 --> 00:08:04,115
A singular subject gets a singular verb.

184
00:08:04,135 --> 00:08:06,690
A plural subject gets a plural verb.

185
00:08:06,710 --> 00:08:10,869
The SAT makes this hard by stuffing
words between the subject and the verb.

186
00:08:10,889 --> 00:08:16,725
So cross out everything in between,
especially phrases starting with words like

187
00:08:16,745 --> 00:08:19,031
of, in, and with.

188
00:08:19,057 --> 00:08:20,057
Try this one.

189
00:08:20,415 --> 00:08:22,216
The collection of letters, photographs,

190
00:08:22,236 --> 00:08:27,564
and diaries donated to the museum by
the artist's family blank a rare look at

191
00:08:27,584 --> 00:08:28,869
her early career.

192
00:08:28,905 --> 00:08:30,155
Pause and try it.

193
00:08:33,392 --> 00:08:36,194
Cross out of letters,
photographs, and diaries.

194
00:08:36,214 --> 00:08:39,229
Cross out donated to the
museum by the artist's family.

195
00:08:39,249 --> 00:08:40,357
What's left?

196
00:08:40,499 --> 00:08:43,301
The collection, blank, a rare look.

197
00:08:43,321 --> 00:08:44,908
The subject is collection.

198
00:08:44,928 --> 00:08:46,392
One collection.

199
00:08:46,463 --> 00:08:47,463
Singular.

200
00:08:47,493 --> 00:08:51,814
Offer, have offered, and
are offering are all plural.

201
00:08:51,993 --> 00:08:54,650
They're tempting because
plural nouns like letters,

202
00:08:54,670 --> 00:08:58,580
photographs, and diaries sit
between the subject and the verb.

203
00:08:58,600 --> 00:09:00,850
The answer is B, offers.

204
00:09:00,897 --> 00:09:02,183
Rule nine.

205
00:09:02,433 --> 00:09:05,755
A pronoun has to match
the noun it replaces.

206
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:08,219
One thing gets it or its.

207
00:09:08,397 --> 00:09:10,862
More than one gets they or their.

208
00:09:10,892 --> 00:09:13,336
The SAT's favorite trap is a group noun,

209
00:09:13,356 --> 00:09:16,035
like a company, a team, or a committee.

210
00:09:16,178 --> 00:09:18,408
On the SAT, those are singular.

211
00:09:18,428 --> 00:09:23,087
After years of research, the company
finally released their first electric

212
00:09:23,107 --> 00:09:24,107
truck.

213
00:09:24,392 --> 00:09:25,499
Wrong.

214
00:09:25,571 --> 00:09:28,428
One company, so it's its
first electric truck.

215
00:09:28,461 --> 00:09:32,226
Whenever pronouns change
across the answer choices,

216
00:09:32,246 --> 00:09:36,496
find the noun the pronoun
points to, and ask one question:

217
00:09:36,961 --> 00:09:39,260
one, or more than one?

218
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,423
Rule ten.

219
00:09:40,994 --> 00:09:44,403
Verb tense has to match the
timeline of the passage.

220
00:09:44,423 --> 00:09:47,903
The choices are usually the
same verb in different tenses,

221
00:09:47,923 --> 00:09:50,600
so your job is to find the time clue.

222
00:09:50,650 --> 00:09:51,650
Try this one.

223
00:09:52,007 --> 00:09:57,130
In 1903, Marie Curie became the
first woman to win a Nobel Prize.

224
00:09:57,150 --> 00:10:01,273
Eight years later, she
blank a second Nobel Prize,

225
00:10:01,293 --> 00:10:03,471
this time in chemistry.

226
00:10:03,544 --> 00:10:04,794
Pause and try it.

227
00:10:08,032 --> 00:10:10,726
The time clues are in 1903,

228
00:10:10,746 --> 00:10:12,675
and eight years later.

229
00:10:13,032 --> 00:10:16,976
That's a specific, finished
moment in the past,

230
00:10:16,996 --> 00:10:18,868
so we need the simple past.

231
00:10:18,888 --> 00:10:21,318
The answer is C, won.

232
00:10:21,414 --> 00:10:23,200
The trap is D, has won.

233
00:10:23,235 --> 00:10:25,715
That tense links the past to the present,

234
00:10:25,735 --> 00:10:29,251
so it can't go with a specific
past time like eight years later.

235
00:10:29,271 --> 00:10:33,235
Wins and will win are
the wrong time entirely.

236
00:10:33,291 --> 00:10:34,505
Rule eleven.

237
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:37,485
Every sentence needs a real main verb.

238
00:10:37,505 --> 00:10:41,700
Words like stretching or being can't
be the main verb by themselves.

239
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,752
Neither can to plus a
verb, like to stretch.

240
00:10:44,772 --> 00:10:45,716
Look at this.

241
00:10:45,736 --> 00:10:50,145
The Great Barrier Reef, stretching
more than 2,000 kilometers along the

242
00:10:50,165 --> 00:10:54,665
coast of Australia, being the largest
coral reef system in the world.

243
00:10:54,735 --> 00:10:55,680
Read it again.

244
00:10:55,700 --> 00:10:57,608
There's no main verb.

245
00:10:57,628 --> 00:11:00,486
Stretching and being can't do the job.

246
00:11:00,564 --> 00:11:04,100
Change being to is, and it works.

247
00:11:04,314 --> 00:11:08,008
The Great Barrier Reef is the largest
coral reef system in the world.

248
00:11:08,028 --> 00:11:10,793
If every choice looks fine on its own,

249
00:11:10,813 --> 00:11:13,564
check whether the sentence
even has a main verb.

250
00:11:13,679 --> 00:11:14,857
Rule twelve.

251
00:11:15,179 --> 00:11:18,587
When a sentence starts with a
describing phrase and a comma,

252
00:11:18,607 --> 00:11:22,345
the very next words have to
name the thing being described.

253
00:11:22,365 --> 00:11:24,845
Running down the street,
my backpack fell open.

254
00:11:24,865 --> 00:11:26,703
That says the backpack was running.

255
00:11:26,723 --> 00:11:30,737
The fix: Running down the street,
I felt my backpack fall open.

256
00:11:30,757 --> 00:11:33,152
Now the runner comes
right after the comma.

257
00:11:33,172 --> 00:11:35,795
Here's an SAT-style version.

258
00:11:35,815 --> 00:11:39,866
Originally developed for the
United States military blank.

259
00:11:39,886 --> 00:11:41,672
Read all four choices.

260
00:11:41,751 --> 00:11:43,001
Pause and try it.

261
00:11:47,239 --> 00:11:51,811
Ask yourself: what was originally
developed for the military?

262
00:11:51,846 --> 00:11:54,454
The Global Positioning System.

263
00:11:54,595 --> 00:11:58,218
So the Global Positioning System
has to come right after the comma.

264
00:11:58,238 --> 00:12:00,377
Only choice A does that.

265
00:12:00,397 --> 00:12:03,697
B and C put people and
the world after the comma,

266
00:12:03,717 --> 00:12:07,111
which would mean they were
developed for the military.

267
00:12:07,468 --> 00:12:11,447
D starts with there are, which
doesn't name anything at all.

268
00:12:11,467 --> 00:12:13,040
The answer is A.

269
00:12:13,072 --> 00:12:14,501
Rule thirteen.

270
00:12:14,715 --> 00:12:19,231
Apostrophes show ownership,
or a shortened word like it's.

271
00:12:19,251 --> 00:12:21,251
They never make a word plural.

272
00:12:21,319 --> 00:12:25,212
Bees, with no apostrophe,
just means more than one bee.

273
00:12:25,319 --> 00:12:28,248
Bee apostrophe s means
belonging to one bee.

274
00:12:28,391 --> 00:12:31,431
Bees apostrophe means
belonging to more than one bee.

275
00:12:31,451 --> 00:12:34,430
And the one to memorize:
it's, with an apostrophe,

276
00:12:34,450 --> 00:12:35,788
means it is.

277
00:12:35,808 --> 00:12:39,165
Its, with no apostrophe,
means belonging to it.

278
00:12:39,214 --> 00:12:40,214
Last question.

279
00:12:40,322 --> 00:12:45,159
When a honeybee finds food, she returns
to the hive and performs a waggle dance.

280
00:12:45,179 --> 00:12:49,976
The direction of the blank tells other
bees which way to fly to reach the food.

281
00:12:49,996 --> 00:12:51,246
Pause and try it.

282
00:12:54,484 --> 00:12:56,178
The passage is about one bee.

283
00:12:56,198 --> 00:12:59,178
A honeybee finds food, and
she performs the dance.

284
00:12:59,198 --> 00:13:01,641
So one bee owns the waggle run.

285
00:13:01,661 --> 00:13:03,820
That's bee apostrophe s.

286
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:05,412
The answer is A.

287
00:13:05,459 --> 00:13:08,760
B has no apostrophe, so
nothing owns the waggle run.

288
00:13:08,780 --> 00:13:12,225
C means more than one bee, but
the passage is about just one.

289
00:13:12,245 --> 00:13:14,724
And D adds an apostrophe to waggle run,

290
00:13:14,744 --> 00:13:16,597
which doesn't own anything.

291
00:13:16,617 --> 00:13:18,276
That's all thirteen rules.

292
00:13:18,296 --> 00:13:20,526
Now, here's how to use them on test day,

293
00:13:20,546 --> 00:13:21,783
in three steps.

294
00:13:21,803 --> 00:13:22,803
Step one.

295
00:13:22,946 --> 00:13:25,175
Look at what changes between the choices.

296
00:13:25,195 --> 00:13:26,374
Punctuation?

297
00:13:26,446 --> 00:13:27,946
It's a Boundaries question.

298
00:13:28,017 --> 00:13:28,925
A verb?

299
00:13:28,945 --> 00:13:30,890
Check agreement and tense.

300
00:13:30,910 --> 00:13:31,817
A pronoun?

301
00:13:31,837 --> 00:13:33,550
Find what it refers to.

302
00:13:33,570 --> 00:13:34,570
Step two.

303
00:13:34,606 --> 00:13:37,586
Find the structure:
the complete sentences,

304
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:40,997
the real subject, the time
clue, or who's doing the action.

305
00:13:41,067 --> 00:13:42,225
Step three.

306
00:13:42,245 --> 00:13:45,674
Eliminate using the rules, not your ear.

307
00:13:45,960 --> 00:13:48,403
If you can't name the
rule an answer breaks,

308
00:13:48,423 --> 00:13:49,817
don't cross it out yet.

309
00:13:49,838 --> 00:13:51,389
And one bonus tip.

310
00:13:51,409 --> 00:13:53,675
If two choices do the exact same job,

311
00:13:53,695 --> 00:13:57,174
like a period and a
semicolon in the same spot,

312
00:13:57,194 --> 00:13:59,246
they can't both be the one right answer.

313
00:13:59,266 --> 00:14:00,531
So neither one is.

314
00:14:00,551 --> 00:14:03,195
Look at what makes the
other choices different.

315
00:14:03,219 --> 00:14:05,341
That's all thirteen, on one screen.

316
00:14:05,361 --> 00:14:07,132
Pause here if you want a screenshot.

317
00:14:07,152 --> 00:14:10,452
Pretty much every grammar question
you'll see tests one of these.

318
